 "summary": "An inward extrude cut of -4.4 mm on the circular top face of a widening conical frustum (SolidCraft's tapered extrude of a radius 1 circle, height 10, taper atan(0.4) = 21.8014 deg, so radius 1 + 0.4*z, 5 at the top) failed: 'not supported yet: the exact fallback for where the bodies meet ... takes planar faces only, and the target has 2 curved faces (cone) and the tool has 2 curved faces (cylinder)'. The cutting cylinder (radius 5, the top face's) touches the cone only along the top rim circle and shares the top face's plane. Geometry is the PR test's (frustum on the z axis; the issue's body sat at x = -6.769, y = 15.405), the frustum built here by revolving its (z, r) section about z, with the PR test's three cutters plus radius 5.1 (clear of the rim) and 4.9 (across it) as controls. Volumes are integrals of the disc area pi*(1 + 0.4*z)^2 over the retained heights, cross-checked by a 2500 x 2500 midpoint grid in (r, z) weighted by 2*pi*r (within 1e-5 mm^3, 5e-4 for the thin ring) and by Gauss-Legendre quadrature (exact). The tolerance is 0.01 mm^3, as in the other boolean cases.",
